   \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{h\\to \\,0}g(h)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-10$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" 13194139533712 true] [17592186247949 1113 #uuid "f18f67d2-c70c-5699-975d-02f1360b3003" 13194139533712 true] [17592186247949 1138 false 13194139533712 true] [17592186247949 1145 "First, let's attempt to evaluate the limit by direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined.\n\nTherefore, to find the limit, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{5sin⁡(2x)}=\\frac{1}{5}csc⁡(2x).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939267/assets/e3f5c484211ef0b9cf5